window.PROBLEM_LESSONS["python-03"] = {"parts": [{"part": 1, "steps": [{"explanation": "Start with two indexes: roles map to permission sets, and users map to role-ID sets. Sets make grants and assignments naturally idempotent, while returning unions and copies keeps callers from mutating the manager's state.", "code": "def __init__(self):\n    self._roles = {}\n    self._user_roles = {}\n    self._parents = {}\n\ndef create_role(self, role_id, permissions=None):\n    if role_id in self._roles:\n        raise ValueError(f\"role already exists: {role_id}\")\n    self._roles[role_id] = set(permissions or [])\n\ndef _role(self, role_id):\n    if role_id not in self._roles:\n        raise KeyError(role_id)\n    return self._roles[role_id]\n\ndef grant_permission(self, role_id, permission):\n    self._role(role_id).add(permission)\n\ndef revoke_permission(self, role_id, permission):\n    self._role(role_id).discard(permission)\n\ndef assign_role(self, user_id, role_id):\n    self._role(role_id)\n    self._user_roles.setdefault(user_id, set()).add(role_id)\n\ndef unassign_role(self, user_id, role_id):\n    self._role(role_id)\n    roles = self._user_roles.get(user_id, set())\n    if role_id not in roles:\n        raise KeyError(role_id)\n    roles.remove(role_id)\n\ndef get_role_permissions(self, role_id):\n    return set(self._role(role_id))\n\ndef get_all_permissions(self, user_id):\n    result = set()\n    for role_id in self._user_roles.get(user_id, set()):\n        result.update(self.get_role_permissions(role_id))\n    return result\n\ndef has_permission(self, user_id, permission):\n    return permission in self.get_all_permissions(user_id)", "rejected": "Separate Role and User objects add identity and mutability without helping this public contract. Class-level registries are especially dangerous because they leak between manager instances."}]}, {"part": 2, "steps": [{"explanation": "Inheritance belongs in the role-permission query. Walking parent links there means every higher-level user query automatically gains transitive inheritance without a second implementation.", "code": "def set_parent_role(self, role_id, parent_role_id):\n    self._role(role_id)\n    self._role(parent_role_id)\n    self._parents[role_id] = parent_role_id\n\ndef get_role_permissions(self, role_id):\n    permissions = set()\n    current = role_id\n    while current is not None:\n        permissions.update(self._role(current))\n        current = self._parents.get(current)\n    return permissions", "rejected": "Copying parent permissions into the child at assignment time looks simple, but later grants and parent replacement would leave stale copies. Traversing the live links preserves one source of truth."}]}, {"part": 3, "steps": [{"explanation": "Collection and matching stay separate. First reuse the effective permission union, then compare against the only four patterns that can cover one resource/action request. Malformed strings cannot match because none equals a valid candidate.", "code": "def has_scoped_permission(self, user_id, resource, action):\n    candidates = {\n        f\"{resource}:{action}\",\n        f\"{resource}:*\",\n        f\"*:{action}\",\n        \"*:*\",\n    }\n    return bool(self.get_all_permissions(user_id) & candidates)", "rejected": "Splitting every stored permission and branching on wildcard positions is longer and easier to get wrong. Generating four candidates makes the finite matching rule explicit."}]}], "reflection": "The transferable idea is to build one authoritative query at the lowest useful layer. Role permissions own inheritance; user permissions compose role permissions; scoped checks consume user permissions. Each new requirement extends the chain instead of creating parallel truth."};
